From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Yuichi Kusakabe <yuichi_kusakabe@jp.honda>
Date: Mon, 17 Aug 2026 00:36:57 +0900
Subject: [PATCH] xenlib: xenstore-srv: input validation and resource bounds


Defects found by adversarial review of the 4.4.1 series. All of them predate
this work - none is a regression from the Zephyr upgrade - but all are
reachable from a guest or from the Dom0 shell, so they are fixed here rather
than left in a tree we are about to run on hardware. 0014 hardened the reply
path; this hardens the inputs and the resource bounds around it.

1. The request payload is not NUL-terminated on the wire, yet every path handler
   treats it as a C string (construct_path -> strlen, deserialize_perms ->
   strlen,
   handle_watch -> indexes past strnlen). `header.len == 0` puts `payload` one
   byte
   past the allocation, and `len` bytes without a NUL walks off the end -
   construct_path() then memcpy()s whatever it found into a xenstore key,
   which the
   guest reads back with XS_DIRECTORY. That is a Dom0 heap disclosure.
   Allocate one
   extra byte and terminate at the boundary, which fixes every handler at once.

2. `xs rm /` from the Dom0 shell, or XS_RM "/" from the toolstack domain,
reaches
   free_node(&root_xenstore): sys_dlist_remove() writes through the NULL
   prev/next
   left by sys_dnode_init(), and k_free() is handed a .bss address. Reject the
   root
   in xss_do_rm() and guard free_node().

3. xss_do_write() links a new node into the tree before setting its
permissions but
   only arms the rollback afterwards, so a perms allocation failure on the
   first new
   node leaves a node with an EMPTY perms list. check_perms() then
   dereferences the
   NULL from SYS_SLIST_PEEK_HEAD_CONTAINER, and so does
   remove_ref_entries_recursive() at teardown. Arm the rollback first, and make
   check_perms() deny instead of crashing if it ever sees one.

4. key_to_watcher() searched the global watch list without filtering by
domain, so
   a matching key+token from another domain was returned: handle_watch()
   overwrote
   that entry's is_relative (corrupting fire_watcher()'s payload trim) and
   created
   no watch for the caller, while handle_unwatch() found the foreign entry,
   skipped
   the removal and still replied "OK". Token comparison was also a prefix match.

5. Watch prefix matching was a bare strncmp, so a watch on .../foo fired for
   .../foobar. Require the match to end on a path boundary.

6. pending_watch_event_list was unbounded and got one entry per matching watch
   rather than per domain, so K watches on one prefix cost K*K output buffers
   per
   write, and a guest that watches "/" and stops reading pins Dom0 heap until
   unrelated domains fail their input allocations. Coalesce per (domain, path)
   and
   cap per domain.

7. process_pending_watch_events() held the global tree lock across the whole
   backlog. With CONFIG_TIMESLICE_SIZE=20 (it was 0 on Zephyr 3.6) this thread
   can
   be preempted mid-drain while holding it, stalling every other domain's
   reads and
   writes. Bound the work per pass, and skip the lock entirely when nothing is
   queued.

8. A watch event whose path+token exceeds XENSTORE_PAYLOAD_MAX made
send_reply_sz()
   emit an XS_ERROR with req_id 0 - the same unsolicited-reply problem 0014
   fixed
   for ENOSYS. Report -EMSGSIZE and let fire_watcher() drop the event.

9. free_node()/remove_recurse() recurse without a depth limit on a 4 KiB worker
   stack, and CONFIG_HW_STACK_PROTECTION is not available on Cortex-A (only
   Cortex-R selects ARCH_HAS_STACK_PROTECTION), so an overflow corrupts the
   neighbouring thread's stack silently. A guest can create ~1500 levels within
   XENSTORE_ABS_PATH_MAX and then remove them. Bound creation depth with the
   existing CONFIG_XENSTORE_TREE_TRAVERSE_DEPTH, which already bounds reads.

Second-round review follow-ups (defects in the first version of this patch):
 - handle_watch()/handle_unwatch() now reject a payload with no "path\0token"
   separator. The input terminator sits at payload[len]; path_len can be len +
   1,
   so `token` pointed one byte past the allocation and key_to_watcher() ran
   strlen()/strcmp() off the heap. handle_set_perms() already had this check.
 - the stored watch token is NUL-terminated. Copying exactly (len - path_len)
   bytes left it unterminated, and fire_watcher() reads it with strlen() and
   memcpy()s the result into the XS_WATCH_EVENT payload sent to the guest - a
   more direct heap disclosure than the one the input terminator closes.
 - the pending-watch drain is no longer gated on !running_transaction. A cap
   plus
   that gate means events queued during a libxl transaction are dropped once the
   cap is reached, and losing a frontend state change hangs `xl create`. The
   transactions here are non-isolating stubs, so there is nothing to defer for.
 - the depth bound is the Kconfig maximum (16) and the worker stack was raised
   to
   8 KiB to match. 8 was exactly the deepest path xen-dom-xs.c writes, leaving
   no
   margin; pvUSB needs 9 and pvSCSI 10. The claim that
   CONFIG_XENSTORE_TREE_TRAVERSE_DEPTH already bounded reads was wrong and the
   comment is corrected.
 - remove_ref_entries_recursive() got the same empty-perms guard as
   check_perms().

Depends on 0006, 0007, 0012, 0014.

Third-round review follow-ups (defects found in this patch's own area):
 - a per-domain watch quota (CONFIG_XENSTORE_MAX_WATCHES_PER_DOMAIN, 128, matching
   the reference xenstored). The pending-event cap this patch added does nothing for
   a guest that drains its ring: `XS_WATCH <path> <fresh token>` in a loop is
   answered OK every time and pins entry+key+token per call until Dom0's 2 MiB heap
   is gone, at which point unrelated domains fail their input allocations.
 - fire_watcher() takes a delivery cursor. It restarts from the head of
   watch_entry_list every call and gives up with -ENOMEM once the domain's
   out-buffer budget is spent, so a domain with more watches on one prefix than
   XENSTORE_MAX_OUT_BUF_NUM got duplicate events forever and the pending entry never
   retired - and because this patch coalesces to one entry per (domain, path), that
   also head-of-line-blocked every later event for the domain.
 - both pending-event producers go through one helper. handle_watch() appended
   directly, bypassing the cap and the coalescing, and notify_watchers() released
   pfl_mutex between counting and appending so the cap could be overtaken.
 - CONFIG_XENSTORE_TREE_TRAVERSE_DEPTH defaults to 16, not 8. The depth reject this
   patch added made the Kconfig default reject legitimate writes: the comment here
   already notes pvUSB needs 9 and pvSCSI 10, so xenlib on its own defaults was
   broken for those device types and only worked because the boards set 16.
 - an empty token is no longer a wildcard in key_to_watcher(). It short-circuited
   the comparison, so `XS_UNWATCH "path\0\0"` removed another token's watch on that
   path and still replied OK.
 - xss_read() NUL-terminates. strncpy() does not when the source fills the buffer,
   and the source is guest-controlled while callers hand the result to atoi() or
   print it with %s.
 - XS_RM fires the removed node's descendants, not just the node. notify_watchers()
   only matches watches whose key is a PREFIX of the path, so removing
   .../device/vbd/<n> told a backend watching .../device/vbd/<n>/state nothing -
   the teardown hang this series exists to remove. XS_RM with an empty path is now
   EINVAL instead of wiping the caller's whole subtree.

Assisted-by: Claude Code:claude-opus-5[1m]
Signed-off-by: Yuichi Kusakabe <yuichi_kusakabe@jp.honda>
Upstream-Status: Pending
